# AI Secretary — infrastructure environment template.
# Copy to `infra/.env` and adjust. NEVER commit the filled-in file.
#
#   cp infra/.env.example infra/.env
#
# Every value has a working default so `docker compose -f infra/docker-compose.yml up -d`
# works unmodified for a first try — but read the notes: you should change the
# Postgres password, and you MUST configure an AI provider in the web UI after start.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SQLite (default in the Dockerfile, simplest): a single file inside the
# app-data volume. Fine for a single-node deployment.
#   DATABASE_URL=file:/app/db/custom.db
#
# Postgres is the production target (the compose file defaults to it).
# Leave DATABASE_URL commented and the app service builds this from POSTGRES_*:
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://ais:change-me@db:5432/ais?schema=public

POSTGRES_USER=ais
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
POSTGRES_DB=ais

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AES-256-GCM key material for encrypting provider API keys and integration
# credentials at rest. If EMPTY, the app generates `.data/secret.key` inside the
# app-data volume on first boot and reuses it.
#
# Generate a strong one:   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# WARNING: changing APP_SECRET later does NOT re-encrypt stored secrets —
# existing provider keys/integration credentials become unusable and must be
# re-entered. See docs/SELF_HOSTING.md.
APP_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Networking (Twilio / inbound webhooks only — not needed for local use)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The absolute public base URL of this deployment, used to build webhook
# callbacks and validate Twilio signatures. Must be reachable from the internet
# if you configure Twilio Voice or accept payment webhooks.
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://receptionist.example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI provider
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is intentionally NO AI credential here and no proprietary AI SDK.
# AI is BYOK: after the app is running, go to Settings → AI Providers and pick
# either a local Ollama server (http://host.docker.internal:11434 from Docker,
# http://localhost:11434 on the host) or an OpenAI-compatible endpoint + key.
# Knowledge/RAG embeddings work out of the box with the built-in local model.
